Well, two off the top of my head would be mail (if you have turned that 
option on) and bones files, I would imagine that there must be others 
that I am not thinking of.

That said, just about any player action can have a dramatic effect on the 
way the game unfolds. You would quickly fall victom to the "butterfly 
effect". For example, if you and someone else (lets call him Frank) both 
enter a level with the same seed, you might expect that you would get the 
same level. But what happens if you have genocided dwarfs, and there was 
supposed to be a dwarf on the level, well that will likely require the 
additional calls to the random number generator, and result in your two 
games ending up in vastly different places.

The easist way to generate similar games would be to somehow generate all 
the levels from the start, save the game, and require everyone to start 
from that save, that way everyone would be using the same layouts and 
pre-generated items atleast.

Also, I'm no expert, but I wouldn't be at all suprised if different 
methods of compiling the source didn't end up using different methods of 
generating random numbers such that just remembering the seed wouldn't do 
a whole lot of good.

There is also the question of what sort of effect complile-time and 
player-set options would have on random number generation.

